From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 21:18:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08073/11362] [outer-loop] wa30's L9 reproduction gate PASSES
 -- 670 actions, reached_level 9, 0.8s

Ran the gate that 16 probe rounds never could. reproduced=True, reached_level=9,
deterministic across three replays in separate processes.

The gate discriminates, which is what makes the pass mean anything: truncating to
604/415/170 actions gives reproduced=False at reached_level 8/6/3 against
claimed_level=9, and each prefix reproduces EXACTLY its documented level
(604->L8, 477->L7, 415->L6, 170->L3, 94->L2).

CORRECTS MY OWN FILING FROM EARLIER TODAY. I wrote that wa30's 9 levels were
"backed by prose" and "cannot be re-verified without redoing the reverse
engineering", and filed it as possible record rot. Wrong on the facts. The action
sequences were complete on disk the whole time and the L1-L8 prefix is described
in its own artifact as "5x gate-verified, replayed identically a 10th time this
session".

What was actually true is narrower: every L4-L9 round self-reports
reproduction_verified=False for the same stated reason -- the gate replays the
whole prefix from reset, so by L9 verifying cost as much as solving, and each
interactive round hit its LIVE step budget first ("810 of 1200 steps used ... the
gate replay costs 604 more"). Offline that budget does not exist and the replay
is sub-second. The verification was never hard; it just never ran outside a
session, so 6 of the 183 headline levels sat unverified ~3 weeks.

The generalisable part: an interactive session can produce a result it
structurally cannot afford to verify, then honestly record verified=False and
move on -- and nothing downstream distinguishes "unverified because it failed"
from "unverified because the session ran out of budget". They look identical.

Registry row now carries the reproduce + action_model fields it lacked (they were
absent, not null). solve_provenance declared honestly as outer_loop_re: wa30 was
solved by an agent probe campaign, not the live agent, so this verifies a prior
solve and confers no live-agent capability claim.

Still open, separately: wa30 has no GameAdapter, so it stays outside offline
induction corpora. reproduce() takes an explicit apply callable, so none was
needed here.
